# Findings gitleaks is right to raise and we are right to keep.
#
# `test/secretScan.test.mjs` proves that our *outbound* scanner refuses to send
# a customer's credential to the provider (`src/secretScan.ts`, PATHWAYS Pathway
# 2 item 8). To prove it, the suite has to contain things shaped like
# credentials — a GitHub token, a Figma token — and a scanner that did not flag
# them would be the broken one. Both scanners are working here; they simply
# disagree about whether a fixture counts, and the fixture is the point.
#
# **Why fingerprints and not `# gitleaks:allow` alone.** gitleaks-action scans
# commit history, so annotating the line today does nothing about the commit
# that introduced it — the diff in 467fd738 still contains the value and always
# will. A fingerprint names one finding in one commit at one line, which is
# exactly the scope of an exception that should never widen. The lines also
# carry inline allows, so editing the file later does not raise them again.
#
# Nothing real belongs in this file. If a genuine credential is ever committed,
# the fix is to rotate it and purge it, never to add a line here.
#
# Checked 2026-08-21: all four are the same two fake values, both defined in the
# S1 fixture table and reused where a suite needs a poisoned field.

# A fake GitHub PAT: the S4 row of the fixture table (97), reused as a poisoned
# skipReason (165) and a poisoned frame label (254).
#
# **No comment here quotes a fixture.** The first version of this file spelled
# out the Figma one, and gitleaks flagged the ignore file — correctly, and on
# the very next commit. Naming the rule and the line is enough; anyone who needs
# the value can open the line the fingerprint points at.
